WEBVTT

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:03.640
Downloaded model files should be treated as supply chain artifacts.

00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:07.880
Provenance matters because unknown sources can create trust, licensing, integrity,

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:08.945
or safety concerns.

00:00:08.945 --> 00:00:13.380
Prefer model files from reputable repositories, maintainers, or organizations.

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:15.940
Read model cards and release notes when they are available.

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:20.125
Understand whether the file is an official release, a community conversion, a fine-tune,

00:00:20.125 --> 00:00:21.640
or an unknown repackaging.

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:25.484
File integrity checks help confirm that the file you have matches the expected version.

00:00:25.484 --> 00:00:28.580
Hashes and checksums do not prove that a model is good or safe,

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:32.920
but they can detect accidental corruption or unexpected modification after download.

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:36.360
When a trusted source publishes hashes, record them with your local notes.

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:40.835
This is especially useful when moving model files between machines or rebuilding a lab.

00:00:40.835 --> 00:00:42.175
Licensing needs real attention.

00:00:42.175 --> 00:00:46.305
Model licenses may affect use, redistribution, modification, commercial deployment,

00:00:46.305 --> 00:00:47.995
attribution, or acceptable use.

00:00:47.995 --> 00:00:51.555
A model that is fine for personal experimentation may not be allowed in a client

00:00:51.555 --> 00:00:53.275
environment or product.

00:00:53.275 --> 00:00:56.080
Local execution does not erase license obligations.

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:59.850
Reviewing the license before building workflows around a model prevents painful surprises

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:00.635
later.

00:01:00.635 --> 00:01:04.540
Local inference can improve privacy because prompts and outputs do not automatically leave

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:05.535
your machine.

00:01:05.535 --> 00:01:07.430
That benefit depends on the surrounding workflow.

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:10.250
Logs, dashboards, synced folders, screenshots, notebooks,

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:12.835
and shared drives can still expose sensitive data.

00:01:12.835 --> 00:01:14.320
Treat models, prompts, outputs,

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:18.060
and runtime configuration with the same care you would apply to any other data-handling

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:18.540
system.
